      "sourceWikitext": "=={{int:filedesc}}==\n{{Information\n|description={{zh|1=阿努比斯小雕像（Statuette Depicting Anubis，新王國）}}\n{{en|1=The god Anubis was depicted in the form of a jackal, or in human with a jackal's head. From the earliest times he was considered the deity in charge of embalming the bodies of the dead and is often depicted in the act of performing this operation.}}\n{{en|1=Anubis also had the task of accompanying the souls of the dead to the Afterlife. The \"Book of the Dead\" shows him holding the deceased's soul by the hand as the heart is weighed during the judgement before Osiris. He was also worshipped as a guardian of necropolises, a role that probably derived from the observation of jackals at Egyptian necropolises in desert locations.}}\n{{en|1=He was particularly venerated in the city of Cynopolis (\"City of Dogs\") in Middle Egypt.}}\n|date=2026-09-25 16:01:44\n|source={{own}}\n|author=[[User:丘崈|丘崈]]\n|permission=\n|other versions=\n}}\n\n=={{int:license-header}}==\n{{self|cc-zero}}\n\n[[Category:Statues of Anubis]]\n[[Category:Recumbent canine (hieroglyph)]]\n[[Category:Statues of the Egyptian New Kingdom]]\n[[Category:Ancient Egyptian statues in the Museo archeologico nazionale (Florence)]]\n[[Category:Interior of the National Museum of History, Taipei]]\n[[Category:2026 photographs of Taipei]]",
